I have looked at the generated code. I believe that the regressions are caused by the JIT failing to inline the method once it was recognized as intrinsic. For example:
```
static double Work()
{
    double arg = 0;
    double sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < 100000000; i++)
    {
        sum += Math.Cos(arg); arg += 1e-8;
    }
    return sum;
}
```
The JIT will recognize `Math.Cos` as intrinsic:
```
In Compiler::impImportCall: opcode is call, kind=0, callRetType is double, structSize is 0
Named Intrinsic System.Math.Cos: Recognized
```
but then does nothing with it and it does not attempt to inline the wrapper.

Windows x86 and x64 failures are unrelated (https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/issues/40057).
Windows arm64 failure is likely due to the removal of fp-model, only relevant change between c860c88 (which was green) and 29a0d18. I don't have the Windows ARM64 device, but going by the [logs](https://helix.dot.net/api/2019-06-17/jobs/127dc993-2c56-447f-ad65-63a54a37049c/workitems/JIT.HardwareIntrinsics/console) and [Validate method](https://github.com/dotnet/runtime/blob/6072e4d3a7a2a1493f514cdf4be75a3d56580e84/src/tests/JIT/HardwareIntrinsics/Arm/AdvSimd/FusedMultiplyAddScalar.Vector64.Double.cs#L544) it seems FusedMultiplyAdd is giving different result than master. Possible repro (it could be something else):

```c#
class Program
{
    static void Main() => System.Console.WriteLine(
        System.Math.FusedMultiplyAdd(0.3683012567312928, 0.5415315770271847, 0.4952658403177121));
}
```

